<Hub pillarId="buying-and-apps">
  <p>
    The buy button is the easy part of the screen. The hard part is knowing who you are paying, what the fee is, and who can spend the crypto after the purchase.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/LearnAndProtect/AdvisoriesAndArticles/understand_risks_of_virtual_currency.html">CFTC</a> tells people not to buy products they do not understand, and to speculate only with money they can afford to lose. It also says many cash-market platforms are not supervised like a bank.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before you create a login, look the company up. <a href="https://msb.fincen.gov/">FinCEN's MSB registrant search</a> lists many money services businesses. The page says a listing is not a recommendation or an endorsement. The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/sites/default/files/LearnandProtect/DigitalAssetRedFlags.pdf">CFTC's red-flag guide</a> also points readers to state regulators, <a href="https://adviserinfo.sec.gov/">AdviserInfo</a>, <a href="https://brokercheck.finra.org/">BrokerCheck</a>, and <a href="https://www.nfa.futures.org/basicnet/">NFA BASIC</a> when someone is selling advice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Type the web address yourself. The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/LearnAndProtect/digitalassetfrauds">CFTC</a> says links and QR codes in messages can be phishing. And crypto you buy is not a bank deposit. The <a href="https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/financial-products-not-insured">FDIC</a> lists crypto assets as products it does not insure.
  </p>
</Hub>
